# From Elliptic-Curve Addition to One Short Signature

A bilinear pairing is a bridge: addition on elliptic-curve torsion becomes multiplication in a target group. Begin by moving the scalars and points in the live identity explorer.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

{{visualization:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the pairing axioms and derived identities</summary>

For $P,Q,R$ in the $n$-torsion subgroup, the pairing satisfies $e(P+Q,R)=e(P,R)e(Q,R)$ and $e(P,Q+R)=e(P,Q)e(P,R)$. Alternation gives $e(P,P)=1$. Induction yields $e(aP,bQ)=e(P,Q)^{ab}$, while expanding $e(P+Q,P+Q)=1$ yields $e(P,Q)=e(Q,P)^{-1}$.

See the [pairing-based cryptography overview](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pairing-based_cryptography) for broader context.
</details>

## Sign and verify

A secret $x$ gives public key $X=xG$; a message hash point $H$ gives signature $\sigma=xH$. Verification compares $e(\sigma,G)$ with $e(H,X)$.

{{algorithm:0}}
{{algorithm:1}}
{{interactive_demo:1}}
{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal why verification determines a unique signature</summary>

Bilinearity proves that the honest signature verifies. If $P\mapsto e(P,G)$ is injective, any verifying candidate has the same pairing value against $G$ as $xH$, and therefore equals $xH$.
</details>

## From forgery to computational Diffie–Hellman

Program a fresh target message to hash to challenge point $B$ and publish $A=xG$. Every valid fresh-message forgery must equal $xB$, exactly the computational Diffie–Hellman target. Learn more about the [computational Diffie–Hellman assumption](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computational_Diffie%E2%80%93Hellman_assumption).

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the conditional security argument</summary>

Validity gives $e(\sigma^\star,G)=e(B,xG)=e(xB,G)$. Injectivity gives $\sigma^\star=xB$. Therefore, if the adversary's attainable outputs exclude $xB$, they also exclude every valid signature on the fresh programmed message.
</details>

## Aggregate many voices into one point

Curve addition combines every finite signature family into $\Sigma=\sum_i\sigma_i$. The pairing unfolds this point as $e(\Sigma,G)=\prod_i e(H_i,X_i)$.

{{algorithm:2}}
{{interactive_demo:2}}
{{visualization:1}}
{{visualization:2}}
{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the finite-set proof and protocol caveats</summary>

Induct on the signer set. The empty aggregate pairs to $1$. Inserting one signature multiplies the pairing by one new factor. Signature representation is constant at one point, although verification work and auxiliary public data may remain linear. Real multi-signer deployments also need rogue-key defenses such as proof of possession and secure hash-to-curve procedures.
</details>

